{"dir":"in","msg":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"session/update","params":{"sessionId":"a6779c33-8390-4f54-9313-6814056e7843","update":{"content":[{"content":{"text":"The Agent Client Protocol Overview (https://www.philschmid.de/acp-overview)\nAgent Client Protocol (ACP) - Docs by LangChain (https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/acp)\nAgent Client Protocol (ACP): The LSP for AI Coding Agents Explained - Marc Nuri (https://blog.marcnuri.com/agent-client-protocol-acp-introduction)\nAgent Client Protocol (ACP): Use Any Coding Agent in Any IDE (https://www.jetbrains.com/acp/)\nZed — Agent Client Protocol (https://zed.dev/acp)\nOverview - Agent Client Protocol (https://agentclientprotocol.com/protocol/v1/overview)\nACP (Agent Client Protocol) (https://ai-sdk.dev/providers/community-providers/acp)\nIntroduction - Agent Client Protocol (https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/introduction)\nagent-client-protocol - crates.io: Rust Package Registry (https://crates.io/crates/agent-client-protocol)\nWhat is Agent Client Protocol? (https://explainx.ai/dictionary/agent-client-protocol)\nThe Agent Client Protocol (ACP) is an open standard for connecting code editors to AI coding agents. Here's what the search turned up:\n\n## What it is\n- ACP standardizes communication between code editors/IDEs and coding agents, and it works for both local and remote setups.\n- People often compare it to the Language Server Protocol (LSP). The goal is to end the old 1:1 coupling where each editor needed its own custom integration for each agent.\n- One overview calls it similar to MCP but for agent to client (UI) communication.\n\n## Origins and backers\n- Zed introduced ACP in August 2025, and JetBrains joined shortly after. JetBrains says it was built by JetBrains and Zed.\n- ACP is open source under the Apache license.\n- Cursor has adopted it too: Cursor is now available as an AI agent inside JetBrains IDEs through the Agent Client Protocol.\n\n## How it works\n- ACP is a JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol. Clients, meaning editors like Zed, JetBrains or Obsidian, manage the user environment. Agents such as Claude Code and Gemini CLI handle the thinking and run the tools.\n- Local agents run as sub-processes of the code editor and talk to it via JSON-RPC over stdio. Remote agents can run in the cloud and connect over HTTP or WebSocket, but full support for remote agents is still in progress.\n- A typical session goes like this: the client sends initialize to set up the connection, authenticates if the agent requires it, then creates a session with session/new or resumes one with session/load. It sends user messages with session/prompt, and the agent sends back session/update notifications as it makes progress.\n- The protocol reuses MCP's JSON formats where it can, but adds custom types for agentic coding features like showing diffs.\n\n## Ecosystem and SDKs\n- **LangChain:** Deep Agents can be exposed over ACP. That lets you use custom deep agents with any ACP-compatible client, so your editor can provide project context and get rich updates back.\n- **Vercel AI SDK:** a community provider connects ACP agents like Claude Code, Gemini CLI and Codex CLI to the AI SDK.\n- **Rust:** an `agent-client-protocol` crate (v2.2.0) lets you build clients that talk to ACP agents, proxies that add capabilities to existing agents, and agents that respond to prompts.\n\n## Watch out for the acronym\nThree different protocols have used the \"ACP\" acronym, and search results still mix them up. Besides the Agent Client Protocol, there's the Agent Communication Protocol, an agent-to-agent protocol linked to IBM Research / BeeAI, and the Agentic Commerce Protocol from OpenAI and Stripe for AI-driven checkout.\n\nThe official docs are at agentclientprotocol.com.","type":"text"},"type":"content"}],"sessionUpdate":"tool_call_update","status":"completed","toolCallId":"toolu_01CNWoc4UGHsHKz79EdvwG3E"}}}}
